SUDO(1)                     User Commands

NAME
    sudo, sudoedit - run a command or edit files as another account

SYNOPSIS
    sudo [-bEHknPS] [-g group] [-p prompt] [-u name] command [argument ...]
    sudo [-kn] [-u name] -i | -s [command]
    sudo -e [-kn] [-u name] file ...
    sudo -l [-U name] | -v | -k | -K | -V

DESCRIPTION
    sudo is sudo 1.9.17p1, compiled unmodified from third_party/sudo with
    the sudoers policy linked into the program. It runs command as the
    account name, root by default, when /etc/sudoers permits it, and asks
    for the password of the caller first. The password is checked
    against /etc/shadow, read from the terminal without echo, and three
    wrong answers end sudo. The first run of each account prints a short
    lecture before the prompt. sudo is set user id root.

    After a correct password sudo writes a time stamp in /var/run/sudo/ts
    and asks no password again for five minutes, as long as the command
    comes from the same terminal and the same login session. The command
    runs with the environment reset to a few variables of the caller and
    with PATH set to the secure_path of /etc/sudoers. Every command and
    every refusal is recorded in /var/log/messages through syslog(3).

OPTIONS
    -b          Run the command in the background.
    -E          Keep the environment of the caller, when sudoers allows it.
    -e          Edit the files as the caller with a copy, as sudoedit does,
                and write them back as the target account. The editor is
                taken from SUDO_EDITOR, VISUAL or EDITOR, /bin/edit by
                default.
    -g group    Run the command with group as its primary group.
    -H          Set HOME to the home of the target account.
    -i          Run the shell of the target account as a login shell, or
                command through it.
    -K          Remove every time stamp of the caller.
    -k          Ignore the time stamp for this command, or remove it when
                no command is given.
    -l          List the commands that the caller, or the account of -U,
                may run.
    -n          Fail instead of asking for a password.
    -P          Keep the supplementary groups of the caller.
    -p prompt   Ask with prompt, where %u stands for the caller and %h for
                the host name. An empty prompt prints nothing.
    -S          Read the password from the first line of standard input
                instead of the terminal. The rest of the input reaches the
                command, which lets a program run a command that reads
                further lines.
    -s          Run the shell named by SHELL, or command through it.
    -u name     Run the command as name.
    -V          Print the version and the configuration.
    -v          Renew the time stamp without running a command.

    sudo on minios has no PAM, no mail to the administrator, no I/O log
    server, no LDAP and no SELinux. visudo and cvtsudoers are not built,
    and root edits /etc/sudoers with an editor directly.

FILES
    /etc/sudoers            the rules, see sudoers(5)
    /var/run/sudo/ts        the time stamps of each account
    /var/db/sudo/lectured   the accounts that have seen the lecture
    /var/log/messages       the log of commands and refusals

EXAMPLES
    sudo pkg install gedit
    sudo -u anna ls /home/anna
    sudo -i
    echo password | sudo -S -k -p '' useradd bob

SEE ALSO
    sudoers(5), doas(1), su(1), id(1), index(7)
